.TH RECOVERAGE 1 "2026-11-13" "recoverage 8.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
recoverage \- coverage dashboard for binary-matching decompilation projects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B recoverage
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]...
.PP
.B recoverage
with no subcommand runs
.BR "recoverage serve" .
Any argument at all, including
.BR \-\-help ,
keeps its own meaning.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B recoverage
serves a local dashboard from canonical Rebrew project metadata, source bindings,
inventory, binaries and link evidence. Run inside the project; no generated
coverage files are required.
.PP
For portable offline viewing, set
.B RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS
to the directory created by
.BR "rebrew coverage export --output snapshots/" .
Preserve old exported history with
.B "rebrew coverage import-history --snapshots db/ --dry-run"
and repeat without
.BR \&\-\-dry\-run
before retiring exports. Status history lives in
canonical function metadata. Keep configured inventory files even under db/.
.PP
.B serve
stops on
.B SIGINT
(Ctrl+C) or
.BR SIGTERM ,
the signal a service manager or a container runtime sends: both unwind the
listener, cancel the deferred browser opener and exit 0.
.PP
Run
.B recoverage config
to print the settings
.B serve
would start with, without binding a port. It reads the environment, so it
preflights a deployment configured through RECOVERAGE_*, not one that passes
.BR serve 's
flags in argv.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Start the recoverage dashboard server.
.TP
.B stats
Print coverage stats as a table, or as JSON with
.BR \-\-json .
The table heads its verdict columns EXACT, RELOC, NEAR and STUB, the words
the dashboard uses, and lists the sections in the order the coverage document
does, which is the load order rebrew writes.
.TP
.B export
Export coverage data to stdout as JSON, CSV or Markdown.
.TP
.B check
Check coverage against a threshold (CI gate).
.TP
.B ops
Answer the relumea backend's recoverage ops over stdin/stdout.
.B ops serve
answers the same ops over HTTP (see
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_TOKEN ).
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
TCP port (default: required; 0 picks a free one; env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_PORT ).
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
Address to bind (default: 127.0.0.1; env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_HOST ).
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
Required: every request
.I project_dir
must resolve inside it (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_WORKSPACE ).
.BI \-\-filestore " DIR"
Retained object store (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_FILESTORE ).
.BI \-\-concurrency " N"
Calls answered at once; the next is refused 503 with Retry-After (default: 2;
env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_CONCURRENCY ).
.BI \-\-allowed\-hosts " HOSTS"
Comma-separated exact additional HTTP Host authorities, including ports (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_ALLOWED_HOSTS ).
.B ops call
.I op
reads one JSON request on stdin and writes the JSON reply on stdout; a
refusal prints an object naming the error, its detail and its status, and
exits 2. The ops are
.BR recoverage.coverage_plan ", " recoverage.coverage_gate ", " recoverage.coverage_map " and " recoverage.coverage_export ;
a request names an absolute
.I project_dir
and the sha256 of a target's binary.
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
confines every request
.I project_dir
to
.I DIR
(env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_WORKSPACE ).
.BI \-\-filestore " DIR"
Retained object store (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_OPS_FILESTORE ).
.TP
.B regen
Refresh the live project view without writing coverage files. Progress and errors go to
stderr. Portable snapshots are read-only; unset RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS to refresh a
project. Normal reads detect input changes automatically.
.TP
.B open
Open the dashboard in a browser. The address opened is the one the listener
holds: a wildcard bind opens through loopback, a named interface opens on
itself, and an IPv6 literal is bracketed.
.B \-\-port
defaults to RECOVERAGE_PORT and
.B \-\-bind
to RECOVERAGE_BIND, the settings
.B serve
runs with.
When nothing accepts a connection at that address it warns on stderr and
opens the tab anyway.
.TP
.B config
Print the configuration
.B serve
would start with, without binding a port. It resolves the environment only
and declares no setting flags, so it preflights a deployment that configures
.B serve
through RECOVERAGE_*; a flag passed to
.B serve
in argv is not a value it can see or check, and is checked when
.B serve
starts.
.TP
.B backup
Copy the portable coverage documents in the
.B RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS
directory into one verified archive, then read it back. Exits 2 when that
variable is unset: canonical project metadata and history belong to Rebrew and
are never backed up or overwritten by this command. Every archive is verified
before this reports success, so a green exit means a restore could read it.
.BI \-\-to " PATH"
Archive path, or a directory for a stamped default name (an existing one, or
any path ending in
.BR / ,
which is created; default:
.BR RECOVERAGE_BACKUP_DIR ,
else a
.B backups
directory beside the snapshot directory). Exits 1 when there is nothing to back
up: an empty archive verifies, and restoring one empties the dashboard. A
destination inside the coverage directory is refused: an archive there shares
its failure domain with the data it protects.
.B \-\-json
writes the verified members and their digests as JSON, for a scheduled job
that reads the result rather than the table.
.TP
.B restore
Put a verified backup's documents back into the selected portable directory. The
archive is verified in full before publication. Each file lands through an
atomic replace, but publication is per file, so stop readers during restore.
Handled failures attempt rollback; incomplete rollback or a process crash can
require manual recovery of retained originals.
.B \-\-force
permits replacing differing archived files and removing stale coverage documents.
Rolling an old archive over a rebuilt tree discards later status transitions, and
.B regen
cannot recover what a rollback drops. Verified safe flat archive members can
include original binaries as well as coverage documents. A corrupt archive, an
unsafe member name and an unreadable file are refused even with force. Requires
.BR RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS .
Offline readers detect the restored files on their own;
.B regen
refuses portable mode. A leftover staging directory from an interrupted
restore is also refused: its
.B previous/
files hold the only copy of the pre-restore documents, so the error names it
for manual inspection rather than overwriting it.
.TP
.B verify-backups
Verify every stamped archive in a backup directory, not only the newest.
Exits 1 naming the first archive that fails, and 0 only when every
stamped archive is one a restore could load. Writes nothing and needs no
.BR RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS .
.B \-\-json
.TP
.B check-backup
Refuse when a backup directory has no fresh stamped archive. Exits 1 when
the directory does not exist, holds no stamped archive, or its newest
archive is older than
.BI \-\-max\-age\-hours
(default 25, one missed nightly run plus slack). The monitor for a scheduled
.B backup
job, which exits 0 only while it runs. Writes nothing and needs no
.BR RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS .
.TP
.B verify
Check every member of a backup archive against its manifest, without
restoring it. The standalone proof for an archive written by an older run,
copied to another volume, or taken from a download, which
.B backup
has not re-read since. Exits 1 naming the member whose size or digest
disagrees, and 0 only when a restore could load it. Writes nothing and
needs no
.BR RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS .
.B \-\-json
writes the verified members and their digests as JSON.
.TP
.B restore\-drill
Prove an archive restores, into a fresh drill directory, serving nothing
live. Verifies the archive in full, restores it into the empty
.BI \-\-into
directory, and reads the restored set back through the coverage loader, so a
restore landing bytes the reader refuses is a drill failure. Refuses a drill
directory that already holds files. Needs no
.BR RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS .
.B \-\-json
writes the restored documents and targets as JSON.
.TP
.B schema
Print the clispec 0.3 JSON document. Reads no project, no token, and no network.
.B schema
.I command
limits the
.B commands
array to that command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-o " " \fIauto|text|json\fR ", " \-\-output " " \fIauto|text|json\fR
Select the output mode before the subcommand.
.B auto
stays text, including when stdout is not a terminal.
.B \-\-json
still requests JSON.
.B \-o text
wins over
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output results as JSON. Same as
.BR "\-o json" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output (overrides
.B NO_COLOR
and
.BR TERM=dumb ).
Every subcommand takes it.
.TP
.B \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-completion
Install shell completion for the current shell.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-completion
Print the completion script for the current shell, to copy or customize it.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help for the command and exit.
.SH "SERVE OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT" "\fR, \fP" \-p " PORT"
Port to serve on, 0-65535 (default: 8001; env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_PORT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ADDRESS"
Interface to bind to (default: 127.0.0.1; use 0.0.0.0 for the LAN; env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_BIND ).
An IPv6 literal such as ::1 is accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-remote
Required with
.BR "\-\-bind 0.0.0.0" :
acknowledges that the unauthenticated API, including raw binary bytes, is
exposed on the network (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_ALLOW_REMOTE ).
Set with a loopback
.BR \-\-bind ,
it acknowledges nothing, and both
.B serve
and
.B config
say so on stderr.
.B \-\-no\-allow\-remote
cancels an exported
.BR RECOVERAGE_ALLOW_REMOTE .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
Do not open a browser automatically.
.TP
.B \-\-regen
Refresh the live project view before starting; writes no coverage files (the only file written is the regen lock).
.TP
.B \-\-cors
Enable CORS processing, for allowlisted origins only (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_CORS ).
.B \-\-no\-cors
cancels an exported
.BR RECOVERAGE_CORS .
.TP
.BI \-\-cors\-origin " ORIGIN"
Origin allowed to read the API cross-origin. Repeatable, and comma-separated
in the environment (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_CORS_ORIGIN ).
An origin that is not
.IR scheme://host[:port]
is refused at startup rather than dropped.
.TP
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
Require this bearer token for every request, as
.BR "Authorization: Bearer <token>" ,
as
.BR "?token=" ,
or by opening the dashboard as
.BI /?token= TOKEN
to set the browser cookie (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_TOKEN ,
which keeps the token out of the process listing).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Log threshold: any name or number
.B logging
knows, such as DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR or CRITICAL (env:
.BR RECOVERAGE_LOG_LEVEL ).
.SH "OTHER SUBCOMMAND OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-target " TARGET" "\fR, \fP" \-t " TARGET"
Target ID. The default is every target.
.TP
.BI \-\-section " SECTION" "\fR, \fP" \-s " SECTION"
Section name. Only
.B check
takes it.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of a table.
.B stats
and
.B check
write the coverage report,
.B config
writes its resolved settings, and
.B export
takes it as the shorthand for
.BR "\-\-format json" .
Combining it with a
.B \-\-format
that is not json is a usage error.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT" "\fR, \fP" \-p " PORT"
Port of the running server.
.B open
only: it takes the same flag, default and RECOVERAGE_PORT as
.BR serve ,
so a deployment that moved the dashboard off 8001 does not need every operator
to remember the new port as well. A port of 0 is refused: it names the free
port the server picked, which is in the banner
.B serve
printed and not something this command can know.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT" "\fR, \fP" \-f " FORMAT"
.B export
output format: json, csv or md (default: json).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-coverage " PERCENT" "\fR, \fP" \-m " PERCENT"
.B check
threshold, 0-100. Required.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every
.B serve
setting flag reads a RECOVERAGE_*
.B \fR environment variable as its default; a flag always wins over the
environment, an unrecognised RECOVERAGE_*
name is a startup error, and so is a value that is not a valid port, boolean,
log level, non-empty string or browser-sendable CORS origin.
.PP
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_PORT
Default for
.BR \-\-port ,
and the port
.B open
opens.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_BIND
Default for
.BR \-\-bind .
An interface address or a hostname. A value carrying a character no address
can hold is refused at startup, so an interface spec and a URL pasted into the
unit file fail with the variable named rather than as a bind error blaming
another instance. A percent sign is legal in one place only: the zone of a
link-local IPv6 literal, which
.B fe80::1
with a zone after it is.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_ALLOW_REMOTE
Default for
.BR \-\-allow\-remote .
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_CORS
Default for
.BR \-\-cors .
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_CORS_ORIGIN
Default for
.BR \-\-cors\-origin .
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_TOKEN
Default for
.BR \-\-token .
Empty means authentication is off. A value carrying surrounding whitespace,
an interior space or a control character is refused at startup: request headers
arrive trimmed, so no client could present it.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_LOG_LEVEL
Default for
.BR \-\-log\-level .
A level name, or one of the numbers
.BR 0 ", " 10 ", " 20 ", " 30 ", " 40 ", " 50 .
Any other number is refused at startup: a threshold no record clears would
log nothing at all.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS
Explicit portable/offline snapshot directory. Leave unset for live project reads;
live services set their working directory inside the project. A file path is a
configuration error. Backup and restore operate only on explicit portable exports.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_PORT
Default for
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-port :
the TCP port to bind; 0 picks a free one. The flag wins when both are set.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_HOST
Default for
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-host :
the address to bind (default: 127.0.0.1). The flag wins when both are set.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_WORKSPACE
Default for
.B ops call
.BR \-\-workspace :
the directory every request
.I project_dir
must resolve inside. The backend sets it when it dispatches an op.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_FILESTORE
Default for
.B ops call
and
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-filestore :
the directory retained object keys resolve inside.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_TOKEN
The bearer token
.B ops serve
requires on every request, at least 32 bytes. It has no flag, so it stays out of the process table.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_CONCURRENCY
Default for
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-concurrency :
the calls answered at once; the next is refused with 503 and Retry-After.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OTLP_ENDPOINT
Default for
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-otlp\-endpoint :
the OTLP/HTTP collector base URL (http or https, no credentials) that receives one server span per request, named
.I POST /<op>
and continuing the caller's traceparent. Empty leaves export off.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_OPS_ALLOWED_HOSTS
Default for
.B ops serve
.BR \-\-allowed\-hosts :
extra exact HTTP Host authorities, with ports, besides the bind address.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_MAX_CONNECTIONS
Cap on concurrent client connections. There is no flag for it: it sizes the
transport rather than selecting a behavior. An integer 1-65536, default 128; a
value outside the range is refused at startup.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_CLIENT_TIMEOUT
Seconds a client socket may take to send a request, per socket operation and
not for a connection's lifetime. Transport, not behavior. An integer 16-86400,
default 120; a value outside the range is refused at startup.
.TP
.B RECOVERAGE_BACKUP_DIR
Directory
.B backup
writes its archive into, and nothing else reads it (default: a
.B backups
directory beside the coverage directory). Never point it inside the coverage
directory: the next backup would copy the previous one, and a restore would
extract an archive as if it were a document.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR check ,
every compared section met the threshold.
.TP
.B 1
Failure. JSON mode writes one stderr line,
.BR {"error":{"kind","message","hint"}} .
For
.BR check ,
a section fell below it; for
.BR stats " and " export ,
a coverage directory holding no
.I coverage\-<target>.toml
or a
.B \-\-target
no build has written; for
.BR open ,
no browser could be launched; for a non-loopback bind without
.BR \-\-allow\-remote ,
the bind was refused.
.TP
.B 2
Misconfiguration: an invalid flag or RECOVERAGE_*
value; and, for
.BR check ,
a coverage directory holding no document or a coverage document that does
not parse, which a gate reports as an infrastructure error rather than as
coverage below the threshold; and, for
.BR regen ,
a missing or unreadable
.I rebrew\-project.toml
and, for
.BR stats ", " export " and " check ,
one that is not readable as a project file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS/coverage\-<target>.toml
Portable coverage documents, read only when
.B RECOVERAGE_SNAPSHOTS
selects them. Live mode reads the canonical Rebrew project inputs and needs no
generated file. The parse cache under XDG_CACHE_HOME serves portable mode only
and is rebuilt from these documents.
.TP
.I backups/coverage\-<timestamp>.tar
The default archive location, beside the snapshot directory: one tar per run
holding the documents and a manifest of their sha256 digests.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS
recoverage                          # start the dashboard on port 8001
recoverage serve \-\-port 3000        # custom port
recoverage stats \-\-json             # machine-readable statistics
recoverage export \-\-format csv > coverage.csv
recoverage check \-\-min\-coverage 50  # CI gate
recoverage regen                     # refresh the live view
recoverage backup                    # verified archive of the documents
recoverage backup \-\-to /srv/backups/nightly.tar
recoverage restore /srv/backups/nightly.tar
recoverage verify /srv/backups/nightly.tar
recoverage open                      # open a running dashboard
recoverage config \-\-json            # what serve would start with
recoverage schema                    # clispec 0.3 document, no project
.RE
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR rebrew (1)
